From: Joonhoe Kim <26rote@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath12k: keep the RX refill ring from running dry

On WCN7860 (Lenovo Legion Tab Gen 5, Kaanapali, PCIe behind an SMMU)
RX stops for good after minutes of heavy traffic (7 minutes of 4+4 TCP
streams on 2.4 GHz here): beacon loss, "failed to flush transmit queue,
data pkts pending 1", and every later connection (both bands, same AP)
gets as far as association and then times out in the 4-way handshake.
Scans keep working. Firmware HTT stats taken in that state:

  SRNG_INFO  RX_SRC_RING_REMOTE_PKT  ring_size 8192 valid 0 hp 4900 tp 4900
             RX_DEST_RING_HOST_DEST  valid 0
  PDEV_RX    host_pkt_buf_ring_empty_cnt 1468398 (refill_cnt 2554227)
             rx_suspend_cnt = rx_abort 563929, fw_ring_ucast_data_msdu 0

The host refill ring was empty. The ring is only replenished from the reap
paths, one buffer for each buffer reaped, so

 - the setup fill posts one ring's worth of buffers (4095), and on chips
   with per-MAC buffer rings (WCN7850 class: rx_mac_buf_ring, two RXDMA
   rings per pdev) the firmware moves them on into the MAC rings. The
   three rings then circulate 4095 buffers out of a 12288-descriptor pool,
   and nothing ever posts more;
 - a buffer that is not posted back (dev_alloc_skb() or DMA mapping
   failure, a descriptor dropped on an error path) is never made up for;
 - ath12k_wifi7_dp_rx_process() skips the replenish when it reaped
   buffers but no complete MSDU (only error pushes, or continuation buffers
   whose last part was not written yet), losing those descriptors and
   leaking the skbs;
 - once the ring is empty RXDMA has nothing to write into, nothing reaches
   the REO destination rings, nothing is reaped, and nothing refills the
   ring. WCN7850-class chips have no host2rxdma low-threshold interrupt
   that would break the loop.

Post the reaped buffers back even when no complete MSDU was reaped, top up
a deficit larger than 1/8 of the ring from the free list in the replenish
path, and add a deferrable 1 s timer that

 - completes the initial fill: it keeps posting from the descriptor pool,
   without a message, until a pool's worth of buffers has gone into the
   ring;
 - after that, refills the ring when it is more than half empty, and warns
   (rate-limited) only when nothing was reaped since the previous run,
   i.e. when RX would have stalled.

Count allocation and mapping failures, missing descriptors, reaps without
an MSDU, the top-ups, the timer refills and the stalls, and show them with
the ring fill level in device_dp_stats, so the next occurrence says where
the buffers went.

With this, the same load ran 30 minutes on 2.4 GHz and 15 minutes on 5 GHz
(8+8 streams) without a stall; the failure counters stayed at 0 and the
free descriptor count did not trend down, so the loss is not a host-side
leak here: the buffers held by the hardware simply fall out of a
circulation that only ever replaces what it reaps.

Signed-off-by: Joonhoe Kim <26rote@gmail.com>
---
Notes: the device results above are for the first revision, which warned
"rx refill ring low" twice on every boot (the firmware taking the initial
buffers into the MAC rings). This revision changes only the timer: a quiet
initial fill up to the pool size, and the warning only without reap
progress. It is compile-tested; the soak is to be repeated with it.

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/debugfs.c b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/debugfs.c
index d54995b7a..12603f05f 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/debugfs.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/debugfs.c
@@ -1032,7 +1032,11 @@ static ssize_t ath12k_debugfs_dump_device_dp_stats(struct file *file,
 	int len = 0, i, j, ret;
 	struct ath12k *ar;
 	u32 center_freq;
-	const int size = 4096;
+	const int size = 8192;
+	struct dp_rxdma_ring *rx_ring = &dp->rx_refill_buf_ring;
+	struct hal_srng *srng;
+	size_t free_descs;
+	int num_free;
 	static const char *rxdma_err[HAL_REO_ENTR_RING_RXDMA_ECODE_MAX] = {
 		[HAL_REO_ENTR_RING_RXDMA_ECODE_OVERFLOW_ERR] = "Overflow",
 		[HAL_REO_ENTR_RING_RXDMA_ECODE_MPDU_LEN_ERR] = "MPDU len",
@@ -1210,6 +1214,31 @@ static ssize_t ath12k_debugfs_dump_device_dp_stats(struct file *file,
 		len += scnprintf(buf + len, size - len, "\n");
 	}
 
+	srng = &dp->hal->srng_list[rx_ring->refill_buf_ring.ring_id];
+	spin_lock_bh(&srng->lock);
+	num_free = ath12k_hal_srng_src_num_free(ab, srng, true);
+	spin_unlock_bh(&srng->lock);
+	spin_lock_bh(&dp->rx_desc_lock);
+	free_descs = list_count_nodes(&dp->rx_desc_free_list);
+	spin_unlock_bh(&dp->rx_desc_lock);
+
+	len += scnprintf(buf + len, size - len,
+			 "\nRX refill ring: %d of %d free, free descriptors %zu\n",
+			 num_free, rx_ring->bufs_max, free_descs);
+	len += scnprintf(buf + len, size - len,
+			 "replenish alloc fail: %u map fail: %u no desc: %u\n",
+			 device_stats->rx_replenish_alloc_fail,
+			 device_stats->rx_replenish_map_fail,
+			 device_stats->rx_replenish_no_desc);
+	len += scnprintf(buf + len, size - len,
+			 "reap without msdu: %u top-up bufs: %u watchdog runs: %u bufs: %u stalls: %u%s\n",
+			 device_stats->rx_reap_no_msdu,
+			 device_stats->rx_refill_topup_bufs,
+			 device_stats->rx_refill_wd_runs,
+			 device_stats->rx_refill_wd_bufs,
+			 device_stats->rx_refill_wd_stalls,
+			 dp->rx_refill_filling ? " (initial fill)" : "");
+
 	ret = simple_read_from_buffer(user_buf, count, ppos, buf, len);
 
 	return ret;
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/dp.c b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/dp.c
